| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio`. Everything runs in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`,
submitted through the executor door. Inputs are opened read-only.

1. Read the gene count matrix (`gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample) and the
   design table (`sample_id`, `condition`, ...). Require that the six sample columns and
   the six design `sample_id` values match exactly; stop with an error otherwise.
2. Transform: `log2(count + 1)` on the length-scaled counts, as they stand. No
   normalisation beyond what the pipeline already applied.
3. Drop genes with zero variance across the six samples.
4. Select the 500 most variable genes (variance of the log2 values; all genes if fewer
   than 500 remain).
5. PCA on samples: centre each gene across samples, no scaling, SVD (numpy). Keep all
   components (at most 5 for six samples).
6. Write the sample coordinates and percent variance explained per component.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`,
   points labelled with sample ID, axes labelled with percent variance. PNG at 200 dpi.

Tools: Python, numpy, pandas, matplotlib as already present in `gars-bio`. Nothing is
installed; if one is missing the analysis is blocked and reported.

